  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nBreezy northwesterly winds in the wake of a weak cold frontal \nboundary will continue through this afternoon before rapidly \ndiminishing this evening. Winds will become light and often \nvariable overnight, while initially clear skies should allow \nfor efficient radiational cooling to start. Though incoming \nhigh clouds will likely limit temperature drops tonight. While \nthe NBM does saturate much of the forecast area to around 100 \npercent, this is likely due to the dynamics of averaging out\nmodels within such a model blend. In other words, deterministic\nguidance maintains a decent dewpoint depression. In addition, \nnear-surface winds aloft remain breezy which usually helps \nprohibit or at least limit fog development. All in all, fog is \ngenerally not expected tonight, though localized shallow fog \ncannot be completely ruled out. Especially in locations where \nincoming high clouds fail to materialize.\n\nLows tonight should generally remain above freezing and in the 30s \nto low 40s. However, similar to fog development, localized freezing \nin traditionally colder spots is entirely possible, especially\nin areas where incoming high clouds fail to materialize. A \nwarming trend is then expected through early next week as a west\nCONUS ridge builds and advects warmer air across the region. \nThis will result in highs ranging from the mid 60s in \nnortheastern North Dakota to the mid 80s in the southwest on \nMonday. Warmer air is then likely to move into eastern North \nDakota on Tuesday, with 70s returning to the southwest. After\nwhich, a slight statewide cooling trend is expected through \nmidweek. Lows are favored to generally remain above freezing \nthrough the next week.\n\nThe NBM maintains a completely dry forecast through next week. \nHowever, WAA could result in a few showers Sunday night into \nMonday. This is a low probability scenario overall due to \ndiscrepancies between models in regard to mid-level moisture \navailability, with an overall dry moisture profile favored. In \naddition, potential shortwave energy Tuesday into Wednesday \ncould produce a round of showers. Beyond Wednesday, the ridge is\nfavored to flatten resulting in northwesterly flow aloft through\nthe remainder of the week, which also frequently produces low \nprecipitation chances that are difficult to forecast well in \nadvance. Bottom line is that mostly dry conditions are expected \nthrough the next week, but a few weak waves could produce light \nrain/showers at times when all is said and done.",
